 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>As well as kicking off the Paris schedule each season with a bang of uninhibited creativity, the IFM graduate show is a fertile hunting ground for fashion houses hungry to spot the next generation of Paris-based design talent. Eagle-eyed executives from LVMH, Kering, and Hermès lined the front row, alongside Celine’s Michael Rider. One talented graduate from the show will receive an internship at Celine, handpicked by Rider.</p><p>Rider, who notably spent the early years of his career as a teacher, is an ideal mentor. “We had the idea that it could be very good to have a big artistic director [involved], and his name came very naturally,” said Thierry Rondenet, who directs the course alongside Hervé Yvrenogeau. “We contacted him and he said yes immediately.”</p><p>The 27 designers that made up the show—already whittled down from a year of 66 students by an expert committee—didn’t make Rider’s choice easy. The looks (6 per designer, 162 total!) that made the long strut down the Les Docks venue represented countries that spanned France to Russia to Vietnam to Sweden, in a show that was as vivid and entertaining as it was diverse. “You have to be relevant in two minutes!” said Yvrenogeau.</p><p>If there was a common thread to be found in this year’s crop, it was a collective yearning to escape from the boundaries of the physical body and an exorcism of personal identity that was expressed in a fabulous clashing of colors and textures, extraterrestrial proportions that toyed with gender norms, and an aggressively deconstructivist approach to tailoring.</p><p>There were dives into Black identity from Lauriane Lothin (133-138) and Adony Biguer (157-162), commentary on the masculine form from Arthur Vieillard (61-66) who made tailored jackets with shoulders as wide as they were long, and anachronistic explorations through time and culture; Hoang Le (127-132) showed space-age Buddhist monks, while Yaouen Chausseblanche (49-54) reinterpreted Breton folklore into a contemporary show of cartoonish knits.</p><p>Saulė Milonaitė (97-102) offered some light relief halfway through with a humorous showing called “bread-a-porter” that mixed baked goods with evening wear, including a croissant transformed into a pair of pants that got the phones standing to attention. Particularly impressive were the many feats of textile manipulation: Jean Oltra (103-108) made a pair of black leather pants that somehow blended into heliotrope velvet, while Hyacinthe Nasli-Bakir (73-78) showed some beautifully variegated prints inspired by photographer Sophie Calle’s Blind.</p><p>Though there is usually a handicraft quality to student shows (by necessity), this year it felt more pronounced and intentional. “Handmade is very important,” said Rondenet. “Doing things by yourself, especially with the fabric, they really want to go deep in this kind of experimentation because it means a lot to them.” So much for the impending doom of AI. “They don’t speak about it,” shrugged Yvrenogeau.</p><p>At the finale, the troop of students came out holding hands with their models, repping slogan T-shirts that read ‘YOUR SILENCE IS POLITICAL’. Indeed, there were no mousy wallflowers to be found here: brash, bold, and ballsy, this lot said it with their chests.</p></div>",
